Okay let's carry on and take a look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:20.960
at the interesting properties we see in&nbsp;
quantum systems with quantum phenomena.
&nbsp;

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:25.600
Particles in quantum mechanics have&nbsp;
many properties. I’ve already mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:28.720
position and momentum, but there are&nbsp;
others which I’ve listed here.
&nbsp;

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:34.720
Spin is a very important property and all&nbsp;
particles are split into one of two categories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:39.440
bosons which have got an integer spin&nbsp;
and can all have the same quantum state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:44.720
and fermions, which have half integer spin and&nbsp;
can’t have the same quantum state as each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:50.640
which is called the Pauli exclusion principle.
Superposition is a property when a particle has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:55.920
probability of being in many different states, for&nbsp;
example, two different places at the same time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:59.840
This simply means that it’s wavefunction&nbsp;
has values in two different places,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:05.040
although when you measure it it will turn up&nbsp;
in just one place. Being in multiple places at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:10.240
once is confusing if we think of particles, but&nbsp;
is completely natural when we think of waves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:15.520
for example any point on the surface of the ocean&nbsp;
is a superposition of thousands of waves.
&nbsp;

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:21.920
You may have heard of Schrodinger's Cat is a&nbsp;
popular description of superposition, although not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:27.200
a very helpful one as it was originally designed&nbsp;
to show how absurd quantum mechanics seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:33.380
as cat’s can’t be alive and dead at the same time.&nbsp;
Which is true, but not because superposition is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:39.600
not real, we now know large things like cats loose&nbsp;
their quantum behaviour because of decoherence.
&nbsp;

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:45.360
Decoherence happens when a quantum object&nbsp;
interacts with an environment and its quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:50.960
behaviour is lost to that environment. Decoherence&nbsp;
is what takes us from the quantum realm to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:55.920
world that we inhabit, and you break the coherence&nbsp;
of quantum objects when you make a measurement.
&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:00.880
Entanglement is where the wavefunction of&nbsp;
the two or more particles interact and mix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:06.320
causing them to become a single quantum object.&nbsp;
This means that the properties of the different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:12.240
particles will be correlated even if they&nbsp;
are separated a large distance. This concept,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:17.680
where the wavefunction that describes a particle&nbsp;
is spread far away from the particle is known as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:22.240
non-locality, and is another thing that doesn’t&nbsp;
happen in the world that we experience.
&nbsp;

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:27.520
There are several other interesting phenomena&nbsp;
which only happen in quantum systems which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:32.480
include: quantum tunnelling - the ability for&nbsp;
particles to cross narrow barriers because their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:38.000
wavefunction penetrates through. Superconductivity&nbsp;
- the ability for electrons to move with zero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:44.080
resistance at low temperatures, and superfluidity&nbsp;
- allowing fluids to flow with zero viscosity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:50.240
There’s also the quantum hall effect which is&nbsp;
the quantisation of conductance in 2D materials,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:54.160
and the casimir effect which is an&nbsp;
attractive force at short distances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.160 --> 00:09:00.400
caused by cutting out large quantum waves&nbsp;
between two plates. An important concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:04.480
when looking at the quantum behaviour of&nbsp;
large systems are phase transitions from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:10.000
one collective behaviour to another. These are&nbsp;
analogous to the different phases of matter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:14.720
solid, liquid and gas but in quantum phase&nbsp;
transitions it is not only temperature and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:19.040
pressure that plays an important role it&nbsp;
can also be applied magnetic field.
&nbsp;

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:23.440
Whenever we discover new interesting&nbsp;
behaviours in physics, one of the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:27.680
questions is can we use this to develop&nbsp;
some new interesting technology?
&nbsp;

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:31.840
And there is a lot of technology that we use&nbsp;
every day which takes advantage of the amazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:36.960
properties of quantum systems. Lasers use a&nbsp;
process called stimulated emission to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:41.360
beams of light with many photons which&nbsp;
all have the same frequency and phase.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:46.480
Atomic clocks keep incredibly accurate time&nbsp;
by using the frequency of light from a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:52.560
specific hyperfine transition in caesium atoms and&nbsp;
are the basis of our global positioning system.
&nbsp;

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:58.160
The band theory of solids describes the energy&nbsp;
levels of electrons in different solid materials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:02.640
and is the basis of the semiconductor industry&nbsp;
which has yielded many different technologies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:07.760
solid state transistors, so basically the&nbsp;
building block of every computer in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:13.920
LEDs which you are probably using to watch&nbsp;
this, CCD’s used in every digital camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:18.160
and solar panels turning the energy&nbsp;
in sunlight into electricity.
&nbsp;

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:22.720
Electron microscopes, scanning tunneling&nbsp;
microscopes and atomic force microscopes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:27.280
allow us to see objects we can’t see with&nbsp;
optical microscopes because they can see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:32.320
smaller than the wavelength of visible light&nbsp;
to resolve objects like viruses or atoms.
&nbsp;

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:38.240
And magnetic resonance imaging techniques are&nbsp;
used in biology and chemistry, for example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:43.840
to look inside our bodies, and these use giant&nbsp;
superconducting magnets to create large magnetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:48.800
fields, and the most sensitive magnetic sensors&nbsp;
in the world called superconducting quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:53.920
interference devices (SQUIDs) whose core&nbsp;
components are a loop of superconducting wire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:57.200
which contain an insulating gap&nbsp;
called a Josephson junction.
&nbsp;

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:02.480
New technologies are being built and&nbsp;
improved in the world of quantum information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:07.040
Quantum cryptography takes advantage of&nbsp;
entanglement to make communication which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:13.120
is extremely secure and forms the basis of the&nbsp;
quantum internet. And quantum teleportation is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:17.920
the ability to perfectly copy the quantum state&nbsp;
of an object from one location to another.
&nbsp;

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:23.760
Quantum bits or qubits are the building blocks&nbsp;
of quantum computers which use superposition and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:28.960
entanglement to create states that are practically&nbsp;
impossible to simulate on a classical computer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:34.000
The challenge is to engineer large groups of&nbsp;
qubits that can stay coherent for long enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:39.040
perform their computations which is not an easy&nbsp;
task. But the potential is huge because their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:44.720
combined superposition means they can explore an&nbsp;
exponential number of states at the same time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:49.040
This puts them in a different complexity class&nbsp;
to the classical computers we use every day.
&nbsp;

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:54.800
There are many exciting applications of quantum&nbsp;
computers but my favourite is quantum simulation:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:59.040
the ability to simulate a quantum system&nbsp;
which would be amazing for things like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:03.600
discovering new materials with entirely new&nbsp;
properties or for solving computationally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:10.800
expensive tasks like protein folding.
Now let's move on to the fields of quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:16.560
physics research. These include condensed matter&nbsp;
physics, quantum biology, cold atom physics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:20.880
quantum chemistry, nuclear physics,&nbsp;
particle physics and theoretical physics.
&nbsp;

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:26.880
Condensed matter physics is the study of large&nbsp;
systems of many atoms in solid or liquid form,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:30.640
and seeking to understand their collective&nbsp;
behaviour on a quantum level.
&nbsp;

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:35.680
I've placed this here because condensed matter&nbsp;
physics covers a lot of these other fields&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:40.160
and condensed matter theory describes the&nbsp;
quantum behaviour of collections of electrons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:44.240
in solids which explains collective&nbsp;
behaviours like superconductivity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:48.960
and the energy bands of semiconductors.
There are many unsolved problems in condensed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:52.880
matter physics, for example, we still don't&nbsp;
have a theoretical model that explains how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:57.840
high-temperature superconductivity works.&nbsp;
In a way the frontier of condensed matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:03.920
physics is complexity. Because the subject&nbsp;
studies complex combinations of many atoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:08.400
which make many different materials with&nbsp;
different physical and electronic properties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:13.760
the potential avenues for study are basically&nbsp;
endless because the combinations are endless.
&nbsp;

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:20.320
Quantum biology studies the role that quantum&nbsp;
mechanics has in biological systems. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:24.720
many processes in biology that are difficult&nbsp;
to explain without quantum mechanics being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:31.280
involved, like the efficiency of energy transport&nbsp;
in photosynthesis, to magneto-reception in birds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:36.640
how our sense of smell and sight work, and&nbsp;
how enzymes speed up chemical reactions.
&nbsp;

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:42.080
Cold atom physics evolved from condensed matter&nbsp;
physics and studies gasses which are controlled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:47.280
in magnetic or optical traps and cooled to&nbsp;
ultra-low temperatures using laser cooling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:51.440
and other cooling techniques. 
Cold atom physics studies many exotic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:56.640
phases of matter like Bose-Einstein Condensates&nbsp;
and Rydberg matter, and looks at their behaviour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.640 --> 00:14:02.400
like quantum phase transitions, quantum spin&nbsp;
systems and many more. Cold atom experiments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:07.520
can also be used as quantum simulators and&nbsp;
quantum sensors like gravity sensors.
&nbsp;

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:13.520
Because quantum mechanics describes the behaviour&nbsp;
of electrons in atoms, it also describes the basic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:19.440
rules of chemistry. The Schrodinger equation is&nbsp;
used to describe electronic structure of atoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:25.120
and how molecules are bonded and move called&nbsp;
molecular dynamics. Solving the quantum mechanics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:30.000
of molecules is a very computationally intensive&nbsp;
task and so computational techniques are very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:34.800
important to quantum chemistry a popular approach&nbsp;
is a method called quantum monte carlo.
&nbsp;

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:40.480
Nuclear physics is the study of the nucleus&nbsp;
of the atom and the ways nuclei can join in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:46.480
nuclear fusion or split apart in nuclear&nbsp;
fission, and the particles and energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:50.800
involved in these nuclear reactions.
Applications of nuclear physics include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:56.000
nuclear power, nuclear weapons, nuclear&nbsp;
medicine, and techniques such as MRI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:02.000
ion implantation and radiocarbon dating.
Particle physics evolved from nuclear physics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:06.480
and is focused on understanding what the&nbsp;
fundamental particles of the universe are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:11.280
and how they interact. Experiments in particle&nbsp;
physics are performed in large particle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:15.760
accelerators where high energy particles&nbsp;
are smashed together to make new particles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:20.640
out of the collision energy. This is why this&nbsp;
field is also known as high energy physics.
&nbsp;

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:26.400
The standard model of particle physics describes&nbsp;
all of the fundamental particles we know of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:30.800
which we’ve discovered over many decades,&nbsp;
the last of which was the Higgs boson. 
&nbsp;

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:35.200
A useful tool for visualising particle&nbsp;
interactions are Feynman diagrams which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:37.760
show what happens when particles collide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:42.400
and they simplify the equations of quantum&nbsp;
field theory into much simpler pictures.
&nbsp;

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:47.200
The theories of the standard model include&nbsp;
quantum electrodynamics which describes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:52.400
the electromagnetic force. Electroweak&nbsp;
interactions which include the weak force,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.400 --> 00:15:56.560
and quantum chromodynamics which&nbsp;
also describes the strong force. 
&nbsp;

00:15:56.560 --> 00:16:01.360
In the standard model, these are all quantum&nbsp;
field theories where particles are understood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:08.240
to be excitations of quantum fields which also&nbsp;
govern how they interact with each other.
&nbsp;

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:13.440
There may be other particles out there that we&nbsp;
haven’t discovered yet. People have proposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:18.000
that dark matter might be made of particles&nbsp;
called weakly interacting massive particles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:23.600
and the large discrepancy between the weak force&nbsp;
and gravity known as the hierarchy problem may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:30.080
be solved by supersymmetric particles.
So the frontier of particle physics is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:35.840
try and work out new ways to explore the landscape&nbsp;
of potential particles at very high energies.
&nbsp;

00:16:37.280 --> 00:16:41.440
Now onto the field of quantum theory&nbsp;
which, although I’ve put it in this box,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.440 --> 00:16:46.560
it really covers this whole map with specific&nbsp;
implementations in each of the fields.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:50.640
Here are some specific aspects of&nbsp;
quantum theory worth knowing about.
&nbsp;

00:16:51.280 --> 00:16:56.000
The core of quantum physics are the postulates of&nbsp;
quantum mechanics that set the ground rules.
&nbsp;

00:16:56.560 --> 00:17:01.120
The path integral formulation of quantum&nbsp;
mechanics is a very elegant way of calculating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:06.560
the motion of particles by integrating over every&nbsp;
possible path that the particle can take.
&nbsp;

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:12.560
And Hilbert spaces are a useful tool to describe&nbsp;
all of the possible states of a quantum system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:17.440
in a giant multidimensional space.
The symmetries of quantum mechanics are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:22.000
an important part of the theory to tell us the&nbsp;
conservation rules which are basically the rules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:26.960
of particle interactions: what should come out of&nbsp;
a particle interaction based on what goes in.
&nbsp;

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:32.640
This is where we hit the limits of our current&nbsp;
knowledge of quantum physics. But there are two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:38.320
main areas of theoretical work looking beyond&nbsp;
the existing models. Firstly the interpretations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:43.440
of quantum mechanics are attempts to resolve the&nbsp;
counterintuitive implications of the wavefunction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:49.120
and quantum gravity aims to reconcile quantum&nbsp;
field theory with general relativity to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.120 --> 00:17:53.280
a grand theory of everything.
The core of the interpretations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:57.440
quantum mechanics is the measurement problem.&nbsp;
When we make a measurement on a quantum object&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.440 --> 00:18:01.760
it’s wavefunction suddenly changes when we&nbsp;
detect the particle. And the laws of quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.760 --> 00:18:06.960
mechanics don’t contain any explanation of what&nbsp;
is actually happening to the wavefunction at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:12.880
instant of measurement. We also don’t know&nbsp;
if the wavefunction is actually real or not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:16.400
And these conceptual problems in quantum&nbsp;
mechanics are what the interpretations of quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.400 --> 00:18:20.480
mechanics attempt to explain.
For many years the default approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.480 --> 00:18:25.520
was the Copenhagen interpretation, but other&nbsp;
popular interpretations include pilot wave theory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:30.880
the many worlds interpretation and quantum&nbsp;
bayesianism and many others. It is too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.880 --> 00:18:34.800
much to get into all of them, but I’ve made&nbsp;
another video which covers this ground.
&nbsp;

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:39.200
They are called interpretations because&nbsp;
we don’t yet have any experiments to tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:43.360
which ones are real and which are not, so&nbsp;
really they are a collection of interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:49.520
ideas rather than proper physical theories.
Quantum field theory is the most comprehensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.520 --> 00:18:54.240
description of reality we have, combining&nbsp;
quantum mechanics with special relativity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:59.120
But we know it is not a complete description as it&nbsp;
doesn’t include general relativity and gravity.
&nbsp;

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:03.280
There are many attempts to merge quantum&nbsp;
mechanics and general relativity into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.280 --> 00:19:08.640
grand unified theory everything and the two&nbsp;
main candidates are string theory and loop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:13.920
quantum gravity. String theory is also known&nbsp;
as M-theory which is a theory that unites all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:19.040
different consistent versions of string theory.
It is very difficult to test the theories of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.040 --> 00:19:23.200
quantum gravity because you need to go&nbsp;
to such high energies, you would need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:28.240
to build a particle accelerator like the large&nbsp;
hadron collider, but the diameter of the solar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.240 --> 00:19:33.040
system with detectors the size of jupiter.
So currently, the best hope we have of signatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.040 --> 00:19:38.480
of quantum gravity are from observing high energy&nbsp;
processes in the universe: signatures from the big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.480 --> 00:19:43.360
bang or black holes might give us some clues&nbsp;
and this is an active area of research.
&nbsp;

